# First-launch wizard opens via the palette command and renders its 7
# sections in the current order (2026-08-27: Nerd Font → Keyboard →
# Input → Claude+Codex install → AI billing → AI ghost-text → VSCode
# shim). Interactive rows for sections 1-3 are exercised. Later sections
# fall below the 40-row test viewport, so they aren't asserted (would
# fail with `expect screen contains` even when focused — see comment in
# src/ui/first_launch_overlay.rs).
#
# NOTE this walks sections with `key down`, so it is positional: adding
# a section shifts every assertion after it. #1208 inserting Keyboard at
# slot 2 is what broke it last — if you add another, re-walk the whole
# file rather than patching the first failure.

command first_launch.show
expect screen contains "First-launch setup"

# Section 1 (Nerd Font) is focused on open — visual foundation renders first
# now (was #3 pre-2026-08-17). Sample glyphs + two answer options visible.
expect screen contains "Nerd Font"
expect screen contains "Sample glyphs"
expect screen contains "Render as icons"
expect screen contains "Render as boxes"

# y quick-answers "Render as icons".
key y

# Section 2 = Keyboard (#1208) — the key-arrival probe. Renders a
# checklist of the chords mnml binds for word / line motion; every row
# starts unticked because nothing has been pressed yet. Asserting the
# labels (not the ticks) keeps this independent of what the CI runner's
# terminal actually forwards, which is precisely the thing that varies.
key down
expect screen contains "Keyboard"
expect screen contains "Ctrl+"
expect screen contains "Option/Alt+"

# Section 3 = Input style. Standard is the pre-populated default (from the
# shipped config), so it should already be selected. Cycle to vim via →.
key down
expect screen contains "Input style"
expect screen contains "vim"
expect screen contains "standard"
key right
expect screen contains "vim"

# Section 4 = Claude Code + Codex install badges. Non-interactive (Space
# fires the shell installer Pty pane which we don't want to trigger here).
key down
expect screen contains "Claude Code"
expect screen contains "Codex"

# Section 5 = AI billing preference (per-product routing). Both product
# rows + choice chips render.
key down
expect screen contains "AI billing preference"
expect screen contains "Auto"
expect screen contains "Sub"

# Esc = Ask me later; overlay closes, `[ui] first_launch_complete` stays
# false. Reopen via first_launch.show to prove idempotence.
key esc
expect screen lacks "First-launch setup"
command first_launch.show
expect screen contains "First-launch setup"

# Finish this time — Enter.
key enter
expect screen lacks "First-launch setup"
